Ne5 ( { PH: Here } 16... h5 { can be considered too, when it surprises me that the computer thinks } 17. h4 $5 { is the best answer. } ) 
17. Na4 Ba8 { Protecting b6. } ( { PH: The immediate } 17... Nfd7 $1 { would have been more precise. The problem with } Ba8 { is that White could now have executed a typical tactic: } 18. Nxb6 Qxb6 19. Nf5 Qb8 20. Nxe7 Rxe7 21. Qxd6 Qxd6 22. Rxd6 $14 { White only has two pawns for the piece, but will likely pick up a third and is well positioned. } ) 
18. Bf1 Nfd7 19. Rc2 Bd8 ( { PH: The computer really doesn't like } 19... Bd8 { , and instead suggests } h6 20. Bg1 Ng6 ( 20... g5 { is also a move. For example } 21. h3 Kh7 22. Bh2 Rg8 { and eventually ...g4. } ) 
21. Rb1 Red8 $14 { White is a bit better but there is not much wrong with Black's position. } ) 
20. Bg1 Bc7 21. Rb1 { As the reader may notice, the piece arrangement resembles very closely the previous example. } Bb7 $2 { The c4-c5 break would be imminent after the preparatory move Qc1, but this makes things much easier for White. } ( { The break } 21... d5 $2 { hangs the a6-pawn for no compensation after: } 22. cxd5 exd5 23. Bxa6 Rcd8 24. Qc3 $1 Qa7 25. Bb5 $18 ) 
 ( { Instead, Black could have tried } 21... Kh8 $5 { followed by ...Rg8 and ...g7-g5; } 22. Qc1 $1 { Defending the rook on b1 and preparing c4-c5 } ( { White is not ready to break with } 21... Bb7 21. c5 $2 bxc5 23. Bxa6 ( 22... Bb7 22. bxc5 $4 Qxb1 $19 ) 
cxd4 24. Bxc8 Rxc8 25. Qxd4 { when chances are roughly equal } ) 
Bd8 23. c5 $1 b5 24. Nb2 d5 ( 24... dxc5 25. bxc5 Nc6 26. Nxc6 Bxc6 27. Nc4 $1 { ± followed by a3-a4 pressuring the b5-pawn } ) 
25. exd5 Bxd5 26. a4 $14 { White’s position is preferable because of the vulnerable a6- and b5-pawns. } ) 
22. c5 $1 { White achieves the thematic c4-c5 break under excellent conditions. Notice how powerful the bishops on f1 and g1 become. } b5 { Hoping to keep the game closed. } ( { Preventing c5-c6 with } 22... Qa8 { is met by: } 23. cxb6 Bxb6 24. Nxb6 Nxb6 25. Nxe6 $1 Rxe6 26. Bxb6 $16 ) 
 ( { Also, opening up the position with } 22... dxc5 23. bxc5 bxc5 ( 23... b5 $2 24. c6 $1 { +- } ) 
24. Nxe6 $1 Rxe6 25. Rcb2 Rd6 26. Qe3 Qa8 27. Rxb7 $16 { gives White a big advantage due to the weak a6- and c5-pawns. } ) 
23. c6 $1 { This typical intermediate move must be remembered, as it creates permanent weaknesses in Black’s camp. } ( { Instead } 23. Nb2 $6 { gives away part of the advantage after } Nc6 $1 24. a4 Nxd4 25. Bxd4 dxc5 26. bxc5 Bxh2 27. axb5 axb5 28. Bxb5 $14 { when White is slightly better but the position is complicated. } ) 
bxa4 24. cxd7 ( { Even stronger is } 24. b5 $3 { which is analyzed separately after this game. } ) 
