 { The previous example was a very convincing win for White, but on White’s 24th move a wonderful thematic blow was missed. The strongest continuation was: } 24. b5 $3 { Opening lines and unleashing the full potential of White’s forces. Black is desperate as the following variations illustrate. } ( 24. cxd7 Nxd7 25. Nc6 { was the game continuation, of course. } ) 
Ba8 ( { Or } 24... Nc5 25. bxa6 Nb3 { hoping to block the b-file, but White is ready to sacrifice his queen with: } 26. cxb7 $1 Nxd2 27. Rxd2 $18 { Now the a6- and b7-pawns together with the bishops on f1 and g1 are far stronger than a queen. For instance } Rcd8 28. Rc2 { and White will soon push through a7. } ) 
25. cxd7 Nxd7 { Material is balanced, but White’s coordination is so strong that the game is decided on the spot after: } 26. Nc6 $1 Qb7 ( 26... Bxc6 { is refuted by: } 27. bxc6 $1 Qxb1 28. cxd7 Qxf1 29. Rxc7 $1 { +- } ) 
27. Qc1 $1 { Threatening bxa6 trapping the queen. } a5 ( 27... axb5 { loses to } 28. Na7 $1 h6 29. Nxc8 Rxc8 30. Rxb5 Nb6 31. Bxb6 { . } ) 
 ( 27... Bd8 { offers no relief after: } 28. Nxd8 Rexd8 29. bxa6 Rxc2 30. axb7 Rxc1 31. bxa8=Q $11 Rxa8 32. Rxc1 $18 ) 
28. b6 $1 { Forcing the opening of lines and making the most of White’s ideally placed rooks and bishops. } Bd8 ( 28... Bxb6 { is bad because of: } 29. Ne7 $1 { +- } ) 
 ( { Also } 28... Nxb6 { loses after: } 29. Nxa5 Qb8 30. Ba6 $18 ( { Or } 29... Qa7 30. Bxb6 $18 ) 
 ) 
29. Nxa5 { White is easily winning, and the game could finish after: } Rxc2 30. Qxc2 Qb8 31. Qxa4 Nf6 32. b7 Bxa5 33. Qxa5 Bxb7 34. Ba6 { Winning the bishop, since White is mating after: } Re7 35. Bxb7 Rxb7 36. Rxb7 Qxb7 37. Qd8+ { From the analysis of this position we learn about the destructive power of White’s forces after the queenside has been opened. It is essential to observe how, after this break, White’s rooks on b1 and c2, and bishops on f1 and g1, perform to the very best of their capacity. } 
