[Event "Chess Structures – A Grandmaster Guide"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/1551485/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "11.25"]
[White "10) The Hedgehog"]
[Black "The space disadvantage - and the trading of pieces"]
[Result "*"]

 { Based on the previous examples, it should be clear that White's spatial advantage plays a significant role in hedgehog positions. Most strategy books teach that we should trade pieces when we have less space. Does this mean Black should trade pieces in the hedgehog? The natural reply would be 'Yes' but reality is not as simple, and in many cases the answer is a forthright 'No'. The reason is that Black's chances of counterplay are mostly based on a ...d6-d5 break, which is unlikely to be helpful unless Black can create serious tactical threats. The trading of two, or even one minor piece, severely undermines the potential threat of a ...d6-d5 break. In other words, having traded some minor pieces would give White a more stable centre, hence a lasting positional advantage. }  
 *

[Event "Chess Structures – A Grandmaster Guide"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/2307812/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "11.26"]
[White "10) The Hedgehog"]
[Black "Pavel Eljanov vs. Dmitri Jakovenko"]
[Result "1-0"]

 { Pavel Eljanov – Dmitri Jakovenko, FIDE World Cup, Tromsø 2013 Learning objectives: 1. The position after 18...Rc8 is an example of White’s advantage in the hedgehog once a couple of minor pieces have been traded. 2. The reader should pay close attention to how Black is unable to push ...d6-d5, and hence must remain passive. } 1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 c5 3. g3 e6 4. Nf3 b6 5. Bg2 Bb7 6. O-O Be7 7. d4 cxd4 8. Qxd4 O-O 9. Rd1 d6 10. Bg5 Nc6 11. Qd2 Qb8 12. h3 Rd8 13. Rac1 h6 14. Be3 Rd7 15. b3 Qf8 16. Nd4 Nxd4 17. Bxd4 Bxg2 18. Kxg2 Rc8 19. e4 { This move establishes a hedgehog-type position under favourable circumstances, since the ...d6-d5 break is hard to accomplish and unlikely to succeed. } Ne8 ( { If } 19... Qd8 20. Qe2 Qc7 21. f4 Qc6 22. Rd3 a6 23. a4 $14 { Black will hardly achieve a break, while a kingside attack with g3-g4-g5 has a good chance to succeed. } ) 
 ( { The break } 19... d5 $2 { loses a pawn after } 20. exd5 exd5 21. Bxf6 Bxf6 22. Nxd5 ) 
 ( { Of course } 19... e5 $6 20. Be3 { gives a stable advantage to White, and is similar to the game Tiviakov – Anand from the previous chapter. } ) 
20. Qe2 Bf6 21. Be3 $5 { White is in no hurry to make further exchanges, as they are unnecessary. White enjoys some space advantage, while Black no longer has enough material to create serious tactical threats. Unfortunately, this move is tactically imprecise as it gives Black some chances for a ...d6-d5 break. } ( { A safer option was } 21. Rd3 Bxd4 22. Rxd4 a6 23. Qe3 ( { PH: A straightforward way to an advantage is } 23. a4 Qe7 24. Rcd1 Rb7 25. R1d3 Qc7 26. h4 $16 { and White can push on the kingside while keeping pressure on the d6-pawn, while Black has no counterplay on the queenside. } ) 
Rb7 24. a4 Rbc7 25. Rcd1 $14 { when Black faces problems, in similar style to the game. } ) 
Qe7 ( { The trade } 21... Bxc3 22. Rxc3 { does not help, as after } Qe7 ( 22... d5 $2 { is refuted by } 23. exd5 exd5 24. Bxh6 $1 { ± since } gxh6 $2 { loses to } 25. Qg4 ) 
23. Rcd3 $14 { White dominates the board and his bishop is far stronger than Black’s knight. He may proceed with the plans a4-a5 or f4-g4-h4-g5 with a big advantage in either case. } ) 
22. Rd3 $6 { An imprecision which could have allowed Black to free his position. } ( { If } 22. f4 Bxc3 23. Rxc3 d5 $1 { and Black releases his position. } ) 
 ( { Better though was } 22. Rc2 { since the try } Bxc3 23. Rxc3 d5 $2 ( { or } 23. Rd3 Nf6 $5 24. Bc1 { followed by Rcd3 } ) 
 { is now refuted by } 24. Rcd3 Nf6 25. exd5 exd5 26. Qf3 { when White is simply winning a pawn. } ) 
Rdd8 $6 { Missing an opportunity. } ( { A better try was } 22... Bxc3 23. Rcxc3 d5 $1 { as after } 24. cxd5 Rxc3 25. Rxc3 exd5 26. exd5 Rxd5 27. Rc8 $14 { White’s advantage is rather small. } ) 
23. Qd2 Qb7 { Threatening ...Bxc3 followed by ...Qxe4. } ( { Now } 23... Bxc3 { is met by } 24. Rdxc3 $1 d5 25. cxd5 Rxc3 26. Qxc3 exd5 27. e5 $1 { ⩲ with a structural advantage. } ) 
24. f3 Be5 25. a4 a6 26. Rd1 Bf6 { White’s position is rock solid, and Black has nothing better than waiting. } ( { If } 26... Nc7 $6 { attempting ...b6-b5 , then } 27. Bd4 $1 { highlights the weakness of the d6-pawn: } Bxd4 28. Rxd4 { And now White is winning a pawn after: } b5 ( { or } 28... Ne8 29. e5 $1 { ± } ) 
29. cxb5 axb5 30. Rb4 $16 ) 
 ( { PH: Black could have tried } 26... Nf6 { , insinuating the ...d5 break, and then } 27. h4 Nd7 { intends ...Nc5 with good counterplay. } ) 
27. h4 { Advancing on the kingside; White intends to follow up with g4-g5. } Rc6 28. Bd4 $1 { White is now ready to attack on the kingside with g4-g5, therefore he must first remove Black’s bishop from f6. } ( { But not } 28. g4 $6 Bxh4 { when things are far from clear. } ) 
Bxd4 29. Rxd4 Rdc8 30. g4 Rc5 31. Rd3 ( { The try } 31. g5 { is worthy of consideration, but after: } Qe7 ( { instead } 31. Rd3 hxg5 32. hxg5 Qe7 33. f4 { is very dangerous for Black, for example } b5 34. Rh1 bxc4 35. b4 $1 R5c7 36. Qe3 Qd7 37. Qh3 Kf8 38. Qh8 Ke7 39. Qg8 $1 { followed by Rh8 with deadly threats } ) 
32. f4 g6 { The game remains complex. } ) 
Qe7 32. Qf2 { Black’s lack of an active plan is unnerving. White may simply speculate with multiple threats, while Black is always responsible for calculating whether concrete threats, such as f3-f4 or g4-g5, are working. Black did not resist the pressure and unnecessarily weakened his position with: } g5 $6 ( { Better was } 32... Qb7 $1 { though after } 33. Qe1 $5 { ⩲ White keeps all his options open } ( { but not } 32... g5 32. f4 $2 b5 $1 { ⇆ when Black frees his position } ) 
 ) 
33. hxg5 hxg5 34. Qd4 $1 { ± Black is now in serious trouble, due to the threat of Rh1. } R8c6 ( { The try } 34... Qf8 { would be met by: } 35. a5 $1 bxa5 ( 35... Rxa5 $6 36. Qxb6 ) 
36. Na4 R5c6 37. e5 dxe5 38. Qxe5 $16 ) 
35. Na2 { Threatening Nb4xa6. } b5 ( { If } 35... a5 { the b5-square is weakened and after } 36. Nc3 Nc7 37. Nb5 Nxb5 38. axb5 Rc7 39. Rh1 e5 40. Qxd6 { the endgame is won for White. } ) 
36. Nb4 Rc8 ( { Or } 36... Rb6 37. cxb5 axb5 38. a5 Rb8 39. Rc3 { when the passed pawn and excellent piece coordination should secure a win for White. } ) 
37. Nxa6 bxc4 38. Nxc5 cxd3 39. Nxd3 $18 { The connected passed pawns give White a decisive advantage, which Eljanov converted in convincing fashion: White won. Final remarks 1. The absence of two minor pieces did not solve Black’s space problem, but it did deprive him of the ...d6-d5 break. 2. The d6-pawn proved a serious weakness throughout the game. Black’s main chance to survive was based on the ...d6-d5 break, which White should not have allowed when he played the imprecise 21. Rd3. } 
 1-0

