 { Alexander Galkin – Aleksander Delchev, Ohrid 2001 Learning objective: This fragment of a game is an additional example of the ...Rg8 and ...g7-g5 plan. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nb5 d6 6. c4 Nf6 7. N1c3 a6 8. Na3 b6 9. Be2 Bb7 10. O-O Be7 11. Be3 Ne5 12. f3 O-O 13. Qd2 Qc7 14. Rfd1 Rac8 15. Rac1 Qb8 { The game has developed as usual and now the real fight begins. } 16. Qe1 Bd8 17. Qf2 $6 { White’s play is imprecise. } ( { A better move order was } 17. Kh1 Bc7 18. Qg1 Ned7 { where White has an extra tempo compared to the game, and after } 19. Nc2 $14 { we have a typical hedgehog position. } ) 
Ned7 18. Kh1 Bc7 19. Qg1 { Protecting the h2-pawn. } ( { PH: Was Qg1 necessary? It's a lazy move, just automatically keeping h2 protected. Let's check: } 19. Nc2 d5 20. cxd5 exd5 21. Nxd5 Nxd5 22. exd5 { , and now if } Bxh2 $2 23. f4 { the bishop is just lost. } ) 
 ( { Considering the way the game developed, Qg1 was a clumsy move. If } 19. Nc2 Kh8 20. Nd4 Rg8 { , then } 21. g4 { would have been a good way to block Black's kingside play. } ) 
Kh8 $5 { Black announces his intention to play ...Rg8 followed by ...g7-g5-g4. } 20. Rc2 ( { A useful piece of advice for players of the white pieces is the following: you should be ready to meet } . Rc2 g7-g5 { with c4-c5 opening the queenside with counterplay. Therefore, the move } 20. b4 $5 { makes perfect sense, and after: } Rg8 21. Na4 Ba8 { 22.Rd2 g5 } 23. c5 $1 { ⩲ White is just in time, and the subsequent complications will probably favour White due to Black’s weakened kingside. } ) 
Rg8 21. Rcd2 g5 22. Qf2 Rg6 23. Nc2 ( { An alternative was: } 23. Bd3 $5 { (threatening e4-e5) } Rg7 ( { but not } 23. Nc2 Ne5 $6 24. Bxb6 g4 25. Bxc7 Qxc7 26. Be2 gxf3 27. Bxf3 Nfg4 28. Qd4 $14 { when Black does not have enough compensation } ) 
24. h3 h5 25. Bd4 Rcg8 26. Nc2 Qd8 $5 { ∞ The break ...g5-g4 is a double-edged weapon due to White’s well-placed bishops. } ) 
Rcg8 { Black’s position remains solid and flexible; he could play ...g5-g4 or just prepare it further with ...h7-h5. } 24. h3 h5 25. Bd4 R8g7 26. b4 Qg8 $5 { Further reinforcing the ...g5-g4 break, though this might just be unnecessary. } ( { Black was perfectly ready for: } 26... g4 $1 27. h4 ( { the opening of the h-file after } 26... Qg8 26. hxg4 $2 { only benefits Black: } hxg4 28. f4 Rh6 29. Kg1 Bxe4 $1 30. Nxe4 Nxe4 31. Bxg7 Kxg7 32. Qe3 d5 $1 { -+ } ) 
Qd8 $5 { The h4-pawn will soon become a serious weakness. } ) 
27. Kg1 g4 28. fxg4 ( { Keeping lines closed with } 28. h4 { does not help much after } gxf3 29. Bxf3 Qd8 { as Black is much better due to the weak pawns on e4 , g2 and h4 . } ) 
 ( { The try } 28. hxg4 hxg4 29. f4 { is nearly suicidal after } Qh7 30. Kf1 g3 31. Qf3 e5 32. Bg1 Rh6 ( { or } 32. fxg4 Rg4 $5 ) 
 { (and Black’s attack is deadly.) } ) 
hxg4 29. Bxg4 ( { After } 29. h4 g3 30. Qf3 e5 31. Be3 Rh7 { White cannot even defend the h4-pawn: } 32. Bg5 $2 Rxg5 33. hxg5 Rh1 $1 34. Kxh1 Qh7 $19 ) 
e5 30. Be3 Nxg4 31. hxg4 Rxg4 $17 { Black has achieved another dream hedgehog position and he imposed his advantage in 12 more moves: Black won. Final remarks 1. Generally speaking, protecting the h2-pawn with Bg1 is somewhat healthier than protecting it with Qg1. However, if White wishes to use the queen for this purpose, he certainly should not lose a tempo by playing Qe1-f2-g1 as in the game. 2. Important: White should be ready to meet ...g7-g5 with a c4-c5 break. 3. Once again, after Black accomplishes the ...g5-g4 break, things do not look good for White in practically every variation. } 
 0-1

