 { Miguel Munoz Pantoja – Julio Granda Zuniga, La Roda 2013 Learning objectives: 1. This game is an example of Black’s ...b6-b5 break when White’s bishop is placed on g2 rather than the f1-a6 diagonal. 2. Something to understand about this position is that White’s queenside plans are no longer a priority in this position, while a kingside attack is a much more suitable idea. 3. Black’s ...d6-d5 break is now nearly impossible, but as we see in this game, the ...b6-b5 break is quite likely to be accomplished. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. c4 b6 4. Nc3 Bb7 5. d4 cxd4 6. Nxd4 d6 7. g3 Ne7 8. Bg2 Nec6 9. O-O Be7 10. b3 O-O 11. Bb2 a6 12. Nc2 Nd7 13. Ne3 Qb8 14. Kh1 Ra7 $5 { This strange-looking move is the start of an interesting plan for Black. He will proceed with ...Ba8, ...Rc7 and ...Rfc8 in order to strengthen the ...b6-b5 break. } ( { A healthy option was: } 14... b5 $5 15. f4 ( { or } 14... Ra7 14. cxb5 $6 axb5 16. Nxb5 Ba6 17. a4 Bxb5 18. axb5 Qxb5 $15 { and the b3-pawn is weak } ) 
Bf6 ( { but not } 15... bxc4 $6 16. Nxc4 $14 { when the d6-pawn is vulnerable } ) 
16. a3 Rd8 17. Rb1 Qa7 18. Ng4 bxc4 19. Nxf6 Nxf6 20. bxc4 Rab8 { Black is slightly better since none of White’s standard plans work here and the c4-pawn is somewhat vulnerable. } ) 
15. f4 Ba8 16. a3 { White is unlikely to profit from advancing his queenside pawns. } ( { Instead, White should focus on the kingside with } 16. Qe2 $5 Rc8 17. Rad1 $5 ( { or } 17. g4 b5 18. g5 { with mutual chances } ) 
b5 18. f5 Nde5 19. fxe6 fxe6 20. Bh3 Nd8 21. Ncd5 $5 exd5 22. Bxe5 dxe5 23. Bxc8 Qxc8 24. cxd5 $14 { with a complicated position. } ) 
Rc8 17. b4 Bf8 18. Qd2 Rac7 19. Rad1 Na7 $5 { A daring idea: Black moves away from the centre in order to prepare the ...b6-b5 break followed by recapturing with a knight. This idea however entails some risks. } ( { The break } 19... b5 $5 { is not so favourable, as after } 20. cxb5 axb5 21. Qd3 Na7 { the b5-pawn needs protection, therefore White remains a little better. A possible continuation is } 22. f5 $5 Ne5 23. Qd2 Nac6 24. Rf2 { when Black’s kingside is in danger. } ) 
20. Rc1 $2 { Wasting a crucial tempo and directing energies in the wrong direction. } ( { Far stronger was } 20. f5 $1 { creating serious kingside threats: } b5 $5 ( { Black’s position is risky after } 20. Rc1 Ne5 21. fxe6 fxe6 22. Rxf8 $5 ( { or the computer-like } 22. b5 $1 { ± blocking the knight on a7 } ) 
Rxf8 23. Qxd6 Rc6 24. Qd4 $14 { with pressure along the a1-h8 diagonal } ) 
21. cxb5 ( { PH: } 21. fxe6 fxe6 22. c5 { is the computer's suggestion, intending } dxc5 23. Ng4 { , with the idea of Qg5 followed by Nh6+ etc. Suddenly Black's pieces are all passively bunched up on the queenside. } ) 
Nxb5 22. fxe6 fxe6 23. Ne2 $1 { ± The e6-pawn is particularly weak. } ) 
b5 $1 { Black achieves the key break for his position. White’s position becomes vulnerable due to over-expansion. } 21. cxb5 Nxb5 22. Nxb5 ( { Or } 22. Ne2 Rxc1 23. Rxc1 Rxc1 24. Qxc1 Be7 { with level chances. } ) 
Qxb5 { It is now White who must play for equality, as the e4-pawn has become vulnerable and Black no longer suffers a space disadvantage. } 23. Rxc7 Rxc7 24. Rc1 Rxc1+ 25. Bxc1 $6 ( { Better was } 25. Qxc1 a5 $5 { with mutual chances. } ) 
Nf6 26. Qc2 a5 $1 { Now White is in trouble, as his queenside and e4-pawn are vulnerable. } 27. Qc4 ( { After } 27. bxa5 $2 Qxa5 { White has no satisfactory answer to the threat of ...Qe1: } 28. Qd2 ( 28. Kg1 Qe1 29. Nf1 Nxe4 $19 ) 
Qxd2 29. Bxd2 Bxe4 $17 ) 
 ( { An option was } 27. Bd2 Qe2 28. Qd1 Qxd1 29. Nxd1 axb4 30. axb4 Nxe4 $17 { although only Black can play for a win. } ) 
Qb6 $5 { Keeping more pieces on the board. } ( { The alternative } 27... Qxc4 28. Nxc4 axb4 29. axb4 Bxe4 $17 { gives good winning chances too. } ) 
28. b5 ( { Again after } 28. bxa5 $2 Qxa5 $17 { the threat of ...Qe1 is very unpleasant. } ) 
Nxe4 $1 { This tactical blow reminds us of how many resources Black gains once a central break has been achieved. } 29. Bxe4 d5 30. Qc8 $1 { White realizes he needs activity to stay in the game. } ( { The try } 30. Nxd5 $2 { loses immediately to } exd5 31. Bxd5 Qe6 $1 { making use of two pins. } ) 
Bb7 31. Qb8 dxe4 $17 { Black has a clear extra pawn and faces no problems in imposing his superiority. } 32. a4 { Threatening Ba3. } Qc5 33. Bd2 Qd4 34. Qxb7 Qxd2 35. Qxe4 g6 36. Ng4 $4 { The final mistake. } ( { More stubborn was } 36. Nc4 Qd1 37. Kg2 Qxa4 38. b6 Qb5 $17 { though Black retains a big advantage. } ) 
Qd1+ 37. Kg2 Qxg4 38. b6 Qf5 ( { White resigns as there is no hope after } 38... Qf5 38. Qa8 Kg7 40. b7 { due to } Qd5 $1 { when checkmate is unstoppable. For example: } 41. Kh3 Qh5 42. Kg2 Qe2 43. Kh3 Qf1 44. Kg4 h5 45. Kh4 Be7 { Final remarks 1. White’s most important mistake in this game was not developing a kingside attack with 20. f5. 2. This game illustrates how Black’s ...b6-b5 break weakens White’s queenside and centre. } ) 

 0-1

