[White "10) The Hedgehog"]
[Black "John Bartholomew vs. Timur Gareev"]
[Result "0-1"]

 { John Bartholomew – Timur Gareev, USA 2009 Learning objectives: 1. This game illustrates how Black can carry out a waiting strategy without damaging his position. 2. Black prepares many plans, and speculates with tactical ideas, but does not carry out any of them. 3. Black requires great calculating ability to carry out this strategy, as one slip can give White an overwhelming initiative, probably through a queenside attack. Both sides must constantly check the consequences of both White’s and Black’s breaks in this structure. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nb5 d6 6. c4 Nf6 7. N1c3 a6 8. Na3 Be7 9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O b6 11. Be3 Rb8 { This prophylactic move gives extra protection to the b6-pawn. } 12. Qd2 Bb7 13. Rfd1 Ne5 14. f3 Re8 15. Kh1 { So far both sides follow the standard developing moves. Something to note is White’s awkwardly-placed knight on a3. White would like to transfer this knight to a better square, say after Nc2-d4; but this is not always easy due to the pressure on the c4-pawn. } Bf8 ( { The premature } 15... d5 $2 { would be met by: } 16. Bf4 $1 Bd6 17. cxd5 exd5 18. Nc4 $1 { Quite an unpleasant surprise! } Nxc4 19. Bxc4 dxc4 20. Bxd6 Rc8 21. Qd4 $16 ) 
 ( { PH: The computer says the best move is } 15... h5 $1 { , which can be followed up by ...Ng6 and ...h4, with good counterplay on the kingside. } ) 
16. Bf1 ( { The attempt to improve the knight with } 16. Nc2 { is met by } Qc7 { attacking the c4-pawn, since the natural } 17. b3 $6 ( { better is } 17. Na4 Bc6 18. Nc3 Bb7 $11 ) 
 { is met by } d5 $1 { opening the centre favourably after: } 18. exd5 exd5 19. Nxd5 Nxd5 20. cxd5 Rbd8 21. Bf1 Rxd5 $15 { Black has gained the initiative. } ) 
Qc7 ( { Again, the break } 16... d5 $2 { is met by } 17. Bf4 $1 Bd6 18. cxd5 exd5 19. Nc4 $1 { ± as in the note above. } ) 
17. Rac1 Ba8 18. Qf2 Kh8 19. Na4 ( { If White tries } 19. Nc2 { intending to follow up with Nd4 , then after } Nxc4 20. Bxc4 Qxc4 { the optimistic } 21. Bxb6 $2 { is refuted by: } Qc6 22. Be3 Rxb2 $17 ) 
Qc6 $1 ( { A more standard reply would be } 19... Ned7 { but then White could play } 20. Nc2 $1 { followed by Nd4 , when White’s chances are slightly preferable. } ) 
20. Nc3 ( { The tempting } 20. Nxb6 $2 { is refuted by } Nfg4 $1 { when Black regains the material favourably after } 21. fxg4 Nxg4 22. Qe2 Nxe3 23. Qxe3 Qxb6 24. Qxb6 Rxb6 $17 { with better chances due to the pair of bishops and weakened dark squares. } ) 
Be7 21. Nab1 Rg8 { Black is ready to create active play on the kingside with ...g7-g5. } 22. Bd4 Qc7 23. b4 $1 { A good decision. As mentioned previously, White should try to meet the ...g7-g5 plan with a well-timed c4-c5 break. } Rgc8 $5 { A curious approach; Black was ready to play ...g7-g5, but now simply decides to go for a different plan. It is typically a mistake to waste three moves on a plan and later choose to do something else. But sometimes in the hedgehog Black can afford such a decision due to the amazing flexibility of the structure. } ( { White was well prepared to meet } 23... g5 { with: } 24. Nd2 Rg6 ( { or } 24... g4 25. f4 $16 ) 
25. Na4 Ned7 26. c5 $1 { Breaking on the queenside ! } bxc5 27. bxc5 dxc5 28. Nxc5 Bxc5 29. Bxc5 Nxc5 30. Rxc5 Qe7 31. Rdc1 $16 { When the g5-pawn is more a weakness than a strength. } ) 
24. Na4 Nfd7 25. Be3 Qb7 { Black now seems to be preparing a ...b6-b5 break. } 26. a3 Rc6 27. Nb2 Rbc8 28. Qd2 Qc7 ( { The break } 28... b5 $2 { does not work well because after } 29. cxb5 axb5 30. Nc3 $16 { the b5-pawn is lost. As it turns out, when White’s knight can reach c3 so easily and White’s bishop is on f1 , the ...b6-b5 break is unlikely to work well. White is nearly winning after } Nc4 31. Nxc4 bxc4 32. b5 R6c7 33. a4 { due to the connected passed pawns. } ) 
29. a4 Qb8 { It is amazing how Black continues to wait without causing any deterioration in his position. It seems Black has not done anything, however he has carefully analyzed White’s threats, and is ready to meet them. } 30. b5 $6 { This is not the right timing for this break, as the b6- and d6-pawns do not become targets as they did in the game Flores Rios – Hayrapetian earlier in this chapter. } ( { Better was } 30. Nc3 $5 { and a possible continuation would be } R6c7 31. Qf2 Rd8 32. Be2 { with roughly equal chances. } ) 
R6c7 $1 ( { Worse is } 30... axb5 $6 31. axb5 { since now White has a potential threat Nc3-a4 attacking the b6-pawn. In addition, the potentially weak a4-pawn has been traded. The play could continue } R6c7 32. Nc3 Ra7 33. Nca4 Qc7 34. Qf2 Rb8 35. Ra1 { with a level game. } ) 
31. bxa6 ( { In the event of } 31. Nc3 a5 $1 { White does not have any breaks on the queenside and Black may turn his attention back to the kingside by playing ...Rg8 followed by ...g7-g5 . For example: } 32. Bg5 $5 { Preventing Black’s plan momentarily. } Bf8 33. Be2 h6 34. Bh4 g5 35. Bg3 Nc5 $15 { White’s position is unpleasant in practice, as he does not have a clear-cut plan. } ) 
Nc5 32. Qc2 Ra7 33. Nc3 Rxa6 $15 { Black’s strategy has been successful and his position is now preferable due to his strong knights on c5 and e5. The rest of the game is irrelevant to our purposes, though eventually Black won. Final remarks 1. This game was a test of nerves for both players. Each player attempted to carry out their plans, but found a well-prepared opponent. If Black had played 15...d5 or 23...g5, he would have reached an inferior position. In turn, White lost his patience and broke with 30. b5, which gave him a worse position. 2. This type of position requires great precision in calculation from both sides, since they must constantly check how to prevent or meet their opponent’s breaks. } 
 0-1

